---
import ChapterBrief from "../ChapterBrief.astro";
import TokensGame from "./TokensGame";
import { chapters, paperLink } from "../../lib/chapters";
const index = chapters.findIndex(c => c.slug === "tokens");
const chapter = chapters[index]!, next = chapters[index + 1]!;
const [gage, gpt2] = chapter.related.map(id => paperLink(id)!);
---
<section class="story-section" id="problem"><p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p><h2>Every word needed<br />its own number.</h2><p>A neural network cannot read letters directly. It reads numbers, so text has to be cut into pieces and each piece given an id. In 2015 translation models usually cut text into whole words and kept a fixed list of the most common few tens of thousands.</p><p>Anything outside that list became a single “unknown” token. Names, new words, typos and long German compounds all fell into the same bucket, and a translator cannot translate a word it was never shown.</p></section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea"><p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p><h2>Build rare words<br />out of common pieces.</h2><p>Rico Sennrich, Barry Haddow and Alexandra Birch borrowed a trick from data compression. Philip Gage’s <strong>byte pair encoding</strong> (1994) shrinks a file by repeatedly replacing the most frequent pair of adjacent bytes with a new symbol. Run the same loop over words in a training text and you get a vocabulary of <strong>subword units</strong>: whole words for common ones, smaller pieces for the rest.</p><p>Their paper, posted in August 2015 and presented at ACL in August 2016, showed that translation models using these pieces translated rare words and names better than models that fell back on a dictionary. Any word can now be spelled from the pieces, so nothing is unknown.</p><p>GPT-2 (2019) went one step further and ran BPE over raw bytes. Its 50,257 tokens can spell any text at all, emoji and every script included. The game below uses GPT-2’s real vocabulary, so the numbers on the chips are the ones GPT-2 read.</p>
<figure class="idea-diagram tokens-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 400 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="tokens-diagram-title tokens-diagram-desc"><title id="tokens-diagram-title">Merging the most frequent pair</title><desc id="tokens-diagram-desc">The word “lower” starts as five letters. Merging common pairs turns it into “lo w e r”, then “low e r”, then “low er”.</desc><g text-anchor="middle" font-size="20" fill="currentColor">{[["l","o","w","e","r"],["lo","w","e","r"],["low","e","r"],["low","er"]].map((row, r) => <g>{row.map((piece, i) => { const w = 360 / row.length; return <g><rect x={20 + i * w + 4} y={14 + r * 70} width={w - 8} height="44" rx="8" fill={`var(--era-${["attention","counting","memory","scale"][i % 4]})`} stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/><text x={20 + i * w + w / 2} y={43 + r * 70} fill="var(--on-accent)">{piece}</text></g>; })}</g>)}</g></svg><figcaption>Each merge is learned once from training text, then applied in the same order to anything you type.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="story-section toy-section" id="play"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Play with it</p><h2>Type anything.<br />Watch it turn into tokens.</h2><ChapterBrief slug="tokens" /><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to split text into GPT-2 tokens.</p></noscript><TokensGame client:load /></section>
<section class="story-section" id="mattered"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p><h2>Models read tokens,<br />not letters.</h2><p>Every large language model since reads text this way. Prices, speed and context windows are all counted in tokens, not words. It also explains some odd failures: a model that sees “ strawberry” as one number has no letters to count, so “how many r’s?” is harder than it looks.</p><p>Tokens are not shared out fairly either. GPT-2 learned its merges mostly from English web pages, so English words are cheap while a Chinese character often costs two or three tokens and Arabic text takes about one token per letter. Later models learned larger vocabularies from more languages to close that gap.</p><a class="next-chapter" href={`/chapters/${next.slug}/`}><span>Next · {next.year}</span><strong>{next.title} ↗</strong><span>{next.hook}</span></a></section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper"><summary>Go deeper <span>How GPT-2 turns text into ids</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body"><h3>1. Cut the text into chunks</h3><p>GPT-2 first splits text with a regular expression into words, numbers, punctuation runs and spaces. A space joins the word after it, which is why “ word” and “word” are different tokens.</p><pre><code>'s|'t|'re|'ve|'m|'ll|'d| ?\p&#123;L&#125;+| ?\p&#123;N&#125;+| ?[^\s\p&#123;L&#125;\p&#123;N&#125;]+|\s+(?!\S)|\s+</code></pre><h3>2. Bytes, with visible stand-ins</h3><p>Each chunk becomes its UTF-8 bytes. The first 256 tokens are the 256 possible bytes; GPT-2’s files show unprintable bytes as other characters, so a space appears as “Ġ”. A chip with a dashed border on this page holds only part of a character, shown as hex bytes.</p><h3>3. Merge in rank order</h3><p>GPT-2 learned 50,000 merges, ranked by when they were learned. For each chunk it repeatedly finds the adjacent pair with the best rank and joins it, until no pair on the list is left. Merge number <em>r</em> creates token 256 + <em>r</em>; the last id, 50256, marks the end of a text.</p><h3>Checked against a reference</h3><p>This page’s tokenizer was compared with an independent implementation on more than half a million strings, including every word in the word list, and matched on all of them.</p></div></details>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="sources"><summary>Sources <span>Papers and data</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body"><ol class="source-list"><li><a href={paperLink("sennrich-2016")}>Sennrich, Haddow &amp; Birch · Neural Machine Translation of Rare Words with Subword Units · ACL 2016 (arXiv 1508.07909)</a><p>Section 3.2: byte pair encoding for word segmentation, learned merge operations and how they are applied to new text.</p></li><li><a href={gage}>Gage · A New Algorithm for Data Compression · C Users Journal, February 1994</a><p>The original byte pair encoding: replace the most frequent pair of adjacent bytes with an unused byte, and repeat.</p></li><li><a href={gpt2}>Radford et al. · Language Models are Unsupervised Multitask Learners · OpenAI, 2019</a><p>Section 2.2: byte-level BPE, the rule that stops merges across character categories, and the 50,257-token vocabulary.</p></li><li><a href="https://huggingface.co/openai-community/gpt2">GPT-2 vocabulary and merges (vocab.json, merges.txt) · OpenAI, MIT licence</a><p>The data behind every chip on this page, packed by this site into a small file that loads only when you open this chapter.</p></li><li><a href="https://sources.debian.org/src/scowl/6-2/r/enable/README/">ENABLE word list · public domain</a><p>Used to check that a word in the second challenge is a real English word.</p></li></ol></div></details>
